# Samba Waf cross-answers for aarch64-linux (glibc, Linux kernel).
#
# Waf asks these questions when a configure probe would run a target program.
# Answers were recorded by running the aarch64 probes under user-mode
# emulation for the narrow samba-smbd configuration, then reviewed: every
# answer matches the native x86_64 configure result except the target machine
# name, and the 64-bit, signal, iconv, and fcntl answers are the glibc
# AArch64 Linux values. AOS build sandboxes have no getconf, so its LFS probe
# keeps the native command-not-found result. There is no target kernel at
# build time, so the uname release and version are fixed placeholders; Samba
# uses them only in diagnostics compiled out on Linux.
#
# Configure fails when Waf asks a question missing here and appends it as
# UNKNOWN to the build's working copy. Add reviewed answers for those
# questions when enabling more features or updating Samba.
Checking uname sysname type: "Linux"
Checking uname machine type: "aarch64"
Checking uname release type: "unknown"
Checking uname version type: "unknown"
rpath library support: OK
-Wl,--version-script support: OK
Checking getconf LFS_CFLAGS: (127, "")
Checking for large file support without additional flags: OK
Checking correct behavior of strtoll: NO
Checking for working strptime: OK
Checking for C99 vsnprintf: "1"
Checking for HAVE_SHARED_MMAP: OK
Checking for HAVE_MREMAP: OK
Checking for HAVE_INCOHERENT_MMAP: NO
Checking for HAVE_SECURE_MKSTEMP: OK
Checking for gnutls fips mode support: NO
Checking value of NSIG: "65"
Checking value of _NSIG: "65"
Checking value of SIGRTMAX: "64"
Checking value of SIGRTMIN: "34"
Checking for a 64-bit host to support lmdb: OK
Checking errno of iconv for illegal multibyte sequence: OK
Checking whether POSIX capabilities are available: OK
Checking if can we convert from CP850 to UCS-2LE: OK
Checking if can we convert from UTF-8 to UCS-2LE: OK
vfs_fileid checking for statfs() and struct statfs.f_fsid: OK
Checking whether we can use Linux thread-specific credentials: OK
Checking whether fcntl locking is available: OK
Checking whether fcntl lock supports open file description locks: OK
Checking whether fcntl supports flags to send direct I/O availability signals: OK
Checking for the maximum value of the 'time_t' type: OK
Checking whether the realpath function allows a NULL argument: OK
Checking for ftruncate extend: OK
Checking for readlink breakage: NO
getcwd takes a NULL argument: OK
